			</span></i>
			<span class="entity">(ii A. D.)</span>, etc. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>II.</b>
			<i>trierarch, one who</i> (singly or jointly with other citizens)
				<i>had to fit out a trireme for the public service,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				Ar.
				<u>Ach.</u>
				546
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Th.
				6.31
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				7.13
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				<u>IG</u>
				12.304.36
			</span></i>, al., <i><span class="en">
				22.1.27
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				1609.57
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				1612.133
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				1629.3
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				183
			</span></i>, etc.— <b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τρῐηραρχ-άρχης</span></b>  is a later form, meaning <i>naval
				commander</i> in general, <i><span class="en">
				Gal.
				<u>Thras.</u>
				47
			</span></i>.</div>
